---
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  DIAGRAM LIBRARY — the drawings, offered for reuse under CC BY 4.0.
//
//  Why this page exists: as of 2026-11-03 the site has ONE external backlink,
//  from an auto-directory, and that is the binding constraint on every ranking
//  on it. These twelve SVGs are the only asset here that somebody else has a
//  reason to put on their own page, and the competition for "garage door parts
//  diagram" is auto-generated spam on compromised hosts. A correct, freely
//  licensed set has almost no legitimate competitor.
//
//  The licence is deliberately CC BY and not CC BY-NC. Non-commercial would
//  exclude the garage door companies and trade blogs who are exactly the people
//  likely to embed a spring diagram — which would protect the drawings from the
//  only use that helps us.
//
//  TWO DELIBERATE OMISSIONS, both for the same reason:
//
//  - cost-map is data-driven. Its callouts are matched out of a guide's own
//    costTable by src/lib/costmap.js and the prices render in the LEGEND, not
//    in the drawing. Shipped as a standalone file it would be numbered markers
//    pointing at figures that are not in the file — and a third party could pair
//    them with their own prices. The one-source-of-truth rule that keeps the
//    city and business pages honest applies here too.
//  - hero-door is decorative, aria-hidden and has no accessible name. It is an
//    illustration, not a reference drawing, and has nothing to caption.
//
//  `data-diagram` on each wrapper is the contract with
//  scripts/extract-diagrams.py, which reads the BUILT page and writes the
//  standalone files. The page declares the name and the extractor follows it,
//  so the two cannot drift.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site.js';
import { getDiagram } from '../lib/diagrams.js';

import TorsionSystem from '../components/diagrams/TorsionSystem.astro';
import SpringTypes from '../components/diagrams/SpringTypes.astro';
import PhotoEye from '../components/diagrams/PhotoEye.astro';
import BalanceTest from '../components/diagrams/BalanceTest.astro';
import OpenerTypes from '../components/diagrams/OpenerTypes.astro';
import SpringLife from '../components/diagrams/SpringLife.astro';
import DoorLayers from '../components/diagrams/DoorLayers.astro';
import WindingCone from '../components/diagrams/WindingCone.astro';
import TrolleyCord from '../components/diagrams/TrolleyCord.astro';
import RollingCode from '../components/diagrams/RollingCode.astro';

const COMPONENTS = {
  TorsionSystem, SpringTypes, PhotoEye, BalanceTest, OpenerTypes,
  SpringLife, DoorLayers, WindingCone, TrolleyCord, RollingCode,
};

// The release set, in reading order rather than registry order: anatomy first,
// then the two things that hurt people, then the opener.
//
// `panels` is how many <svg> elements the component renders, which is NOT
// always obvious from its source: BalanceTest draws one <svg> inside a .map()
// and produces three. Get it wrong and the page offers a download URL that was
// never written. scripts/extract-diagrams.py compares these links against what
// it actually extracts and fails on any disagreement, so this list cannot drift
// from the drawings without the pipeline saying so.
const RELEASE = [
  { name: 'torsion-system', panels: 1 },
  { name: 'spring-types', panels: 2 },
  { name: 'winding-cone', panels: 1 },
  { name: 'spring-life', panels: 1 },
  { name: 'door-layers', panels: 1 },
  { name: 'balance-test', panels: 3 },
  { name: 'photo-eye', panels: 1 },
  { name: 'opener-types', panels: 2 },
  { name: 'trolley-cord', panels: 1 },
  { name: 'rolling-code', panels: 1 },
];

// Same guard as the guide template: a name here with no component rendered as
// nothing at all, silently, on the page whose entire purpose is the drawings.
const items = RELEASE.map(({ name, panels }) => {
  const d = getDiagram(name);
  if (!d) throw new Error(`diagrams.astro: "${name}" is not in src/lib/diagrams.js`);
  const Svg = COMPONENTS[d.component];
  if (!Svg) {
    throw new Error(
      `diagrams.astro: "${name}" names component "${d.component}", which is not imported here.`
    );
  }
  // A single-panel diagram keeps the clean filename; only a multi-panel one is
  // suffixed, matching what the extractor writes.
  const files = panels === 1
    ? [name]
    : Array.from({ length: panels }, (_, i) => `${name}-${i + 1}`);
  return { name, d, Svg, files };
});

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Diagrams', url: '/diagrams/' },
];

const credit = `Diagram by ${SITE.name} (${SITE.domain}), CC BY 4.0`;
const creditHtml =
  `<a href="${SITE.domain}/diagrams/">Diagram by ${SITE.name}</a>, `
  + `<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Garage Door Diagrams, Free to Reuse"
  description="Ten original garage door diagrams — springs, cables, safety sensors, openers and door anatomy — drawn in-house and free to reuse with credit."
  canonical="/diagrams/"
>
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />

    <article style="margin-top:1rem;">
      <h1 style="margin:1rem 0 .5rem;">Garage door diagrams, free to reuse</h1>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Every drawing on this site is original, authored by hand as SVG, and made for
          one purpose: to show someone standing in front of a door that will not open
          what they are actually looking at. There are no photographs here and never will
          be — a photo of a van or a technician next to a listing would imply we have
          seen that business, and we have not.
        </p>
        <p>
          You are welcome to take any of them. They are more useful on your page than
          sitting on ours.
        </p>

        <div class="highlight-box">
          <h2 style="margin-top:0;font-size:1.05rem;">The license, in plain terms</h2>
          <p style="margin:.4rem 0;">
            These diagrams are released under
            {' '}<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0</a>.
            You may use them anywhere, including commercially, including on a competing
            site, and you may modify them. The single condition is credit with a link.
          </p>
          <p style="margin:.4rem 0;">Copy this wherever you use one:</p>
          <p style="margin:.4rem 0;">
            <code style="display:block;padding:.6rem;background:var(--paper);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:4px;font-size:.82rem;overflow-wrap:anywhere;">{creditHtml}</code>
          </p>
          <p style="margin:.4rem 0 0;font-size:.85rem;" class="muted">
            Plain text, if you cannot use a link: {credit}
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          If you modify a drawing, please say so in the credit. The reason is not vanity:
          these are used to explain which part of a garage door is under enough tension
          to injure someone, and we would rather an altered version not be read as ours.
        </p>
      </div>

      {items.map(({ name, d, Svg, files }) => (
        <section class="section-sm" data-diagram={name}>
          <h2 style="font-size:1.2rem;">{d.caption.split(/[.:]/)[0]}</h2>
          <Figure caption={d.caption} legend={d.legend || []}>
            <Svg id={`dl-${name}`} />
          </Figure>
          <p style="font-size:.85rem;">
            {files.map((f, i) => (
              <>
                {i > 0 && <span class="muted"> · </span>}
                <a href={`/diagrams/${f}.svg`} download>Download {f}.svg</a>
              </>
            ))}
            <span class="muted">
              {files.length > 1 ? ' — one file per panel. ' : ' — '}
              SVG, scales to any size, colors baked in.
            </span>
          </p>
        </section>
      ))}

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What is not here, and why</h2>
        <p>
          The cost map that appears on the repair-cost guide is missing on purpose. Its
          numbered markers are matched against that guide's own price table and the
          figures render in the legend beside it, not inside the drawing. Released as a
          standalone file it would be a set of markers pointing at prices that are not in
          the file — easy to pair with someone else's numbers, and impossible for us to
          keep honest. Prices belong with the guide that owns them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The homepage illustration is also not here. It is decorative rather than
          explanatory, and there is nothing to label on it.
        </p>

        <h2>Found a mistake?</h2>
        <p>
          These are drawn from manufacturer documentation and trade practice, not from
          site visits. If something in one is wrong, tell us and we will fix the drawing
          rather than argue about it: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
